Centralized vs. Distributed Intelligence in Advanced AI Systems

A Formal Framework for Capability, Coordination, Safety, and Alignment Trade-offs Between Monolithic and Multi-Agent Architectures

Ouadi Maakoul



For Humanity 4

Abstract

This thesis develops a formal mathematical framework for comparing centralized, distributed, and hybrid architectures for advanced AI systems. Building on recent empirical work establishing quantitative scaling principles for multi-agent systems, we provide the missing mathematical foundations: (i) a capability-vector formalism with explicit correlation structure; (ii) a Dec-POMDP formalization separating consumable resources from environmental constraints; (iii) an N-agent extension of the team-decentralisation-loss formalism for the collaboration tax, with context-dilution corrections grounded in Transformer attention mechanics; (iv) a signed spectral branching-process model of error propagation and correction, grounded in Metzler matrix theory; (v) a viability-theoretic characterization of collective alignment with computational approximations via control barrier functions for Multiagent-POMDPs; (vi) an independent conjecture on phase transitions for budgeted multi-agent synergy using effective context windows; (vii) a latency-constrained regime for multiplanetary and extreme-latency AI systems. We derive the quadratic saturation ansatz from the collaboration-tax and dilution models rather than positing it. We position the contribution against existing work on agent scaling laws, scalable oversight, Byzantine fault tolerance, and coordination economics.

---

1. Introduction and Positioning

1.1 Established empirical results

The centralized-versus-distributed question is no longer purely theoretical. Controlled evaluations have established:

· Multi-agent variants degrade sequential reasoning performance by 39–70% across 260 configurations, while improving parallelizable tasks such as financial reasoning by +80.8%.
· Independent agents amplify errors through unchecked propagation; centralized coordination contains this via validation checkpoints.
· Coordination yields diminishing returns once single-agent baselines exceed performance thresholds; the optimal architecture is predictable for 87% of unseen tasks from task structure alone.
· 24–57% of coordination spending is unnecessary for correctness under monotonicity decompositions of organizational tasks.
· The collaboration tax is the team-decentralisation loss of a two-player cooperative game with private information, measured across 32 tasks and 11 models from 7 providers, with the tax structured along two no-exception orderings: capability and task category.
· LLM-based agents demonstrate stronger skepticism when processing erroneous message flows, outperforming traditional agents across topological structures under Byzantine conditions up to 85.7% fault rate.
· The one-way signal delay of 4–22 minutes between Earth and Mars is treated as a design condition rather than an obstacle: the operator transmits goals, and onboard autonomy determines execution.

1.2 What is novel in v0.6

This revision provides:

1. Derivation of the saturation ansatz from the collaboration-tax and context-dilution models (§4.1), replacing the posited quadratic form with a first-principles derivation.
2. Tightened attention-to-value link (§3.2), relating effective mutual information to the team value function.
3. Sharper Metzler spectral bounds (§5.1), noting when the absolute-value surrogate is conservative.
4. Computational approximations for viability kernels (§6.4), with explicit CBF construction from local observations and delayed global constraints for Multiagent-POMDPs.
5. Numerical example (§10.5), demonstrating the quantities in action for small N with known topology.
6. Explicit theorem status table (§11), distinguishing theorems under stated assumptions from modeling choices and conjectures.

