2. Formal Preliminaries

2.1 Capability vector

Definition 2.1 (Capability vector). For system S on task distribution \mathcal{T} and environment \mathcal{E}, the capability vector is

\mathbf{C}(S) = (C_{\text{acc}}, C_{\text{rob}}, C_{\text{gen}}, C_{\text{cal}}, C_{\text{rec}}, C_{\text{eff}}) \in \mathbb{R}^6

where the components denote accuracy, robustness, generalization, calibration, recovery, and sample efficiency respectively.

Remark 2.1 (Correlation structure). These dimensions may be empirically collinear. Let \Sigma_C \in \mathbb{R}^{6 \times 6} be the correlation matrix estimated from benchmark evaluations. Pareto dominance is assessed in the Mahalanobis-transformed space:

\tilde{\mathbf{C}}(S) = \Sigma_C^{-1/2} \mathbf{C}(S)

Definition 2.2 (Pareto dominance). S_1 \succ S_2 iff \tilde{\mathbf{C}}(S_1) \succ \tilde{\mathbf{C}}(S_2) componentwise with at least one strict inequality.

2.2 Separated resource and constraint vectors

Definition 2.3 (Resource vector). The consumable resources of architecture A are

\mathcal{R}(A) = (K_{\text{FLOPs}}, K_{\text{tokens}}, K_{\text{VRAM}})

Definition 2.4 (Constraint vector). The environmental constraints of architecture A are

\mathcal{E}(A) = (\tau_{\max}, B_{\min}, \epsilon_{\max})

where \tau_{\max} is maximum tolerable latency, B_{\min} is minimum bandwidth, and \epsilon_{\max} is maximum error rate.

Definition 2.5 (Matched comparison). Architectures A_1, A_2 are compared under matched budgets iff

\mathcal{R}(A_1) = \mathcal{R}(A_2) \quad \text{and} \quad \mathcal{E}(A_1) = \mathcal{E}(A_2)

componentwise. This is exact equality, not approximation.

2.3 Dec-POMDP formalization

Definition 2.6 (Centralized architecture). A centralized architecture is a POMDP

\mathcal{C} = \langle S, A, T, O, \Omega, R, \gamma \rangle

with a single observation function \Omega: S \times A \to \Delta(O) and a single policy \pi: O \to \Delta(A).

Definition 2.7 (Distributed architecture). A distributed architecture with N agents is a Dec-POMDP

\mathcal{D} = \langle N, S, \{A_i\}_{i=1}^N, T, \{O_i\}_{i=1}^N, \Omega, R, \gamma, \mathcal{G}, \mathcal{M}, \mathcal{V} \rangle

where \mathcal{G} = (V, E) is the communication graph, \mathcal{M} is the communication protocol, and \mathcal{V} is the verification architecture.

Definition 2.8 (Hybrid architecture). A hybrid architecture is

\mathcal{H} = \langle \mathcal{C}_0, \mathcal{D}_1, \dots, \mathcal{D}_k, \mathcal{P}_{\text{auth}} \rangle

where \mathcal{C}_0 is a coordinator POMDP and \mathcal{P}_{\text{auth}} is an authority-allocation policy.

2.4 Communication model

Definition 2.9 (Communication cost). For agents i, j,

\kappa_{ij} = \alpha \cdot \frac{|m_{ij}|}{B_{ij}} + \beta \cdot \tau_{ij} + \gamma \cdot \epsilon_{ij}

where |m_{ij}| is message size, B_{ij} bandwidth, \tau_{ij} latency, and \epsilon_{ij} error rate.

Definition 2.10 (Total coordination cost).

K_{\text{coord}}(\mathcal{D}) = \sum_{(i,j) \in E} \kappa_{ij} + K_{\text{verify}} + K_{\text{sync}}

2.5 Alignment specification

Definition 2.11 (Global alignment specification). Let X_t = (x_1, \dots, x_N) \in \mathcal{X} be the joint state. A global alignment specification is a set \mathcal{S}_{\text{align}} \subseteq \mathcal{X} with viability constraint

X_0 \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{align}} \implies X_t \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{align}} \quad \forall t \ge 0

This is a controlled invariance condition, not a static property.

