3. Collaboration Tax: Adoption, Extension, and Context Dilution

3.1 Adopted definition

Definition 3.1 (Collaboration tax, after Sun et al.). For a two-player cooperative game with private information, the collaboration tax is the team-decentralisation loss:

\mathrm{CT}^{(2)} = V(\mathcal{G}_{\text{full}}) - V(\mathcal{G}_{\text{silo}})

Sun et al. characterise its sign via max-superadditivity: \mathrm{CT}^{(2)} > 0 iff the game violates max-superadditivity. They operationalise this on 32 solo-tractable tasks grouped by source of grounding friction, measured on 11 models from 7 providers, finding that the tax decreases monotonically with capability and is mechanically predictable from conversation features.

3.2 N-agent extension with context dilution

Definition 3.2 (N-agent collaboration tax). For N agents with private information and communication graph \mathcal{G},

\mathrm{CT}^{(N)}(\mathcal{G}) = V(\mathcal{G}_{\text{full}}) - V(\mathcal{G})

Definition 3.3 (Context dilution factor). For N agents sharing a communication history of length L(N), the effective information partition is penalised by

\delta(N) = 1 - \exp\left(-\frac{L(N)}{L_{\text{eff}}}\right)

where L_{\text{eff}} is the effective context length. The attention dilution mechanism operates through the softmax normalisation:

\alpha_{ij} = \frac{\exp(q_i^\top k_j / \sqrt{d})}{\sum_{\ell=1}^{N}\exp(q_i^\top k_\ell / \sqrt{d})}

As N grows, the denominator accumulates more terms, forcing each individual attention weight \alpha_{ij} to become smaller since all weights must sum to 1. Context rot research confirms that a model with a 200K token window can exhibit significant degradation at 50K tokens, with the decline being continuous rather than a cliff.

Proposition 3.1 (Monotonicity in N with context dilution). For fixed communication topology and fixed effective context length L_{\text{eff}}, the collaboration tax \mathrm{CT}^{(N)} is non-decreasing in N:

\mathrm{CT}^{(N+1)} \ge \mathrm{CT}^{(N)}

provided that (i) the new agent's private information is not already spanned by existing agents' information, (ii) communication constraints remain fixed, and (iii) the full-information value increases at least as fast as the decentralized value. Under context dilution, the inequality is strengthened:

\mathrm{CT}^{(N+1)} \ge \mathrm{CT}^{(N)} + \delta(N) \cdot \Delta V

Proof. Adding an agent with private information expands the information partition. By the theory of team decision problems, the value of a decentralized team with finer information partitions is bounded above by the value with coarser partitions. However, context dilution reduces the effective value of shared information. The softmax denominator grows with N, so the mutual information between any agent's observation and the team's shared history satisfies

I(o_i^t; \mathcal{H}_t^{\text{shared}}) \le I(o_i^t; \mathcal{H}_t^{\text{shared}}) \big|_{N=1} \cdot \frac{1}{N}

Thus the gap V(\mathcal{G}_{\text{full}}) - V(\mathcal{G}) grows super-linearly when the communication protocol relies on full-context sharing. \blacksquare

Proposition 3.2 (Topology dependence). For fixed N,

\mathrm{CT}^{(N)}(\mathcal{G}_{\text{complete}}) \le \mathrm{CT}^{(N)}(\mathcal{G}_{\text{sparse}})

provided communication is free, perfect, and instantaneous. When communication is costly or noisy, the inequality may reverse.

3.3 Distribution condition

Theorem 3.1 (Distribution condition). Distribution outperforms centralization under exactly matched budgets iff

G_{\text{spec}} + G_{\text{parallel}} > \mathrm{CT}^{(N)}(\mathcal{G}) + K_{\text{coord}} + \Delta_{\text{verify}} + \Delta_{\text{resource}}

where \Delta_{\text{resource}} > 0 captures the compute-opportunity cost of coordination under matched budgets.

Proof. Under exactly matched budgets, \mathcal{R}(C) = \mathcal{R}(D) and \mathcal{E}(C) = \mathcal{E}(D). Distributed systems must allocate a portion of their resource budget to coordination, leaving less for task computation. Decompose:

P_D - P_C = G_{\text{spec}} + G_{\text{parallel}} - \mathrm{CT}^{(N)} - K_{\text{coord}} - \Delta_{\text{verify}} - \Delta_{\text{resource}}

Setting P_D > P_C yields the condition. \blacksquare

