4. Capability Saturation: Derivation from First Principles

4.1 Derivation of the saturation law

Theorem 4.1 (Derived saturation law). Under the collaboration-tax and context-dilution models, multi-agent performance follows

P_D(N) = p_0 + \alpha(1-p_0)N - \frac{\beta}{2}N^2 + \epsilon

where \alpha, \beta > 0, and the optimal agent count is

N^* = \frac{\alpha(1-p_0)}{\beta}

Proof. From Theorem 3.1, the net gain from distribution is

\Delta P(N) = G_{\text{spec}} + G_{\text{parallel}} - \mathrm{CT}^{(N)} - K_{\text{coord}} - \Delta_{\text{verify}} - \Delta_{\text{resource}}

The coordination cost K_{\text{coord}} and verification cost \Delta_{\text{verify}} grow linearly in N:

K_{\text{coord}} + \Delta_{\text{verify}} = \kappa N + \mathcal{O}(1)

The collaboration tax \mathrm{CT}^{(N)} grows super-linearly due to context dilution (Proposition 3.1):

\mathrm{CT}^{(N)} = \tau_0 + \tau_1 N + \tau_2 N^2 + \mathcal{O}(N^3)

For small N, the quadratic term dominates the super-linear growth. Substituting into the distribution condition:

P_D(N) = P_C + G_{\text{spec}} + G_{\text{parallel}} - \tau_0 - (\tau_1 + \kappa)N - \tau_2 N^2 + \mathcal{O}(N^3)

Setting p_0 = P_C + G_{\text{spec}} + G_{\text{parallel}} - \tau_0, \alpha(1-p_0) = -(\tau_1 + \kappa), and \beta/2 = \tau_2 yields the stated form. The optimum follows from \partial P_D/\partial N = 0. \blacksquare

Remark 4.1. The empirical observation that coordination benefits invert at ~45% single-agent accuracy provides a calibration point: 1 - \beta/\alpha \approx 0.45. This is a testable prediction of the derived law, not a confirmation.

4.2 Communication threshold

Theorem 4.2 (Communication threshold). There exists B^* such that B > B^* \implies P_D > P_C and B < B^* \implies P_D < P_C.

Proof. K_{\text{coord}} is decreasing in B; P_D is increasing in B via \partial \mathrm{CT}^{(N)}/\partial B < 0. Define f(B) = P_D(B) - P_C. At B \to 0, f(B) < 0; as B \to \infty, f(B) \to G_{\text{spec}} + G_{\text{parallel}} > 0. Continuity gives B^*. \blacksquare

