5. Error Propagation and Correction: Signed Spectral Branching Processes

5.1 Signed next-generation matrix

Definition 5.1 (Signed next-generation matrix). Let \mathcal{G} = (V, E) be the agent communication graph. Define

K_{ij} = p_{ij} \cdot r_j

where p_{ij} \in \mathbb{R} is the signed propagation coefficient from agent i to agent j: p_{ij} > 0 for error amplification, p_{ij} < 0 for corrective feedback, and r_j is the expected number of errors agent j generates if compromised.

Remark 5.1. When K has negative entries, the Perron–Frobenius theorem no longer directly applies to the spectral radius. We use the theory of Metzler matrices, where a matrix is Metzler if all off-diagonal elements are non-negative. For a signed matrix K, we bound the spectral radius using the absolute value matrix |K|:

\rho(K) \le \rho(|K|)

If |K| is irreducible and non-negative, Perron–Frobenius applies to |K|, and the cascade threshold is governed by \rho(|K|). When many p_{ij} < 0 (strong corrective feedback), the absolute-value surrogate is conservative; sharper results can be obtained via the spectral abscissa of the Metzler matrix.

5.2 Cascade threshold and blast radius

Definition 5.2 (Cascade reproduction number).

R_c = \rho(|K|)

Theorem 5.1 (Cascade threshold). If R_c < 1, the expected cascade size is finite:

\mathbb{E}[B] = \mathbf{1}^T (I - |K|)^{-1} \mathbf{1}

If R_c > 1, cascades grow without bound with positive probability.

Proof. This is the standard multi-type branching process threshold theorem. By Perron–Frobenius theory for non-negative matrices, if \rho(|K|) < 1, the trivial fixed point is globally stable and \mathbb{E}[B] = \mathbf{1}^T (I-|K|)^{-1} \mathbf{1}. If \rho(|K|) > 1, a nontrivial fixed point exists. \blacksquare

Corollary 5.1.1. For the scalar case with R_c < 1,

\mathbb{E}[B] = \frac{1}{1 - R_c}

5.3 Topology comparison

Corollary 5.2.1 (Topology dependence). For fixed total agents N and homogeneous propagation parameters p, r:

Topology R_c scaling \mathbb{E}[B] scaling
Centralized R_c \propto N \cdot p \cdot r 1/(1 - Npr)
Hierarchical R_c \propto \log N \cdot p \cdot r 1/(1 - pr\log N)
Ring R_c \propto 2pr 1/(1 - 2pr)

Proof. For a centralized topology, the hub connects to all N nodes, so K has row sums N \cdot p \cdot r, giving R_c = Npr. The expected blast radius follows from Theorem 5.1. For hierarchical, each level has bounded degree and the cascade must traverse \mathcal{O}(\log N) levels. For ring, each node has degree 2. \blacksquare

5.4 Verification containment

Theorem 5.2 (Verification reduces R_c). If verification catches fraction q of errors, the effective next-generation matrix becomes

K^{\text{ver}}_{ij} = (1-q) K_{ij}

and

R_c^{\text{ver}} = (1-q) R_c

Proof. If a fraction q of propagated errors are caught and neutralized, the effective propagation probability is reduced by factor (1-q). The spectral radius scales linearly with matrix entries. \blacksquare

Corollary 5.3.1. Containment requires

q > 1 - \frac{1}{R_c}

