6. Collective Alignment as Invariance

6.1 Viability kernel

Definition 6.1 (Viable set). Let \mathcal{S}_{\text{align}} \subseteq \mathcal{X} be the safe set. The viability kernel is

\mathrm{Viab}(\mathcal{S}_{\text{align}}) = \{X_0 \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{align}} : \exists \pi, \forall t \ge 0, X_t \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{align}}\}

Theorem 6.1 (Collective alignment characterization). A multi-agent system is collectively aligned under policy \pi iff the trajectory \{X_t\}_{t \ge 0} satisfies X_t \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{align}} for all t \ge 0. A system admits an aligned policy iff

X_0 \in \mathrm{Viab}(\mathcal{S}_{\text{align}})

Proof. The first statement is the definition of fixed-policy alignment. The second follows from the definition of the viability kernel as the set of initial states from which at least one controlled trajectory remains in the constraint domain. \blacksquare

6.2 Individual vs. collective alignment

Theorem 6.2 (Individual alignment does not imply collective alignment). There exist agents A_1, A_2 and local specifications V_1, V_2 such that

A_1 \models V_1, \quad A_2 \models V_2, \quad \text{but} \quad (A_1, A_2) \not\models V_{\text{global}}

Proof by construction. Let V_1 require that agent 1 never outputs a value exceeding 10. Let V_2 require that agent 2 never outputs a value exceeding 10. Let V_{\text{global}} require that the sum of outputs never exceeds 15. Each agent individually satisfies its local specification (outputs 8 and 8), but the joint output 16 violates the global specification. \blacksquare

6.3 Authority separation

Definition 6.2 (Authority-separated execution). For each action a, define

\mathrm{Execute}(a) = \mathrm{Cap}(a) \land \mathrm{Auth}(a) \land \mathrm{Verify}(a) \land \mathrm{Safe}(a)

Theorem 6.3 (Authority separation reduces blast radius). Separating capability from authority reduces the expected blast radius:

\mathbb{E}[B_{\text{sep}}] \le \mathbb{E}[B_{\text{unsep}}]

Proof. In an unseparated system, any action within the capability set can be executed. In a separated system, execution requires additional authorization and verification gates. This reduces the effective branching factor p_{ij} in K, lowering R_c and hence \mathbb{E}[B] by Theorem 5.1. \blacksquare

6.4 Computational approximations of the viability kernel

The viability kernel is computationally intractable for high-dimensional Dec-POMDP state spaces. We propose two approximation strategies grounded in established theory.

Approach 1: Control Barrier Functions (CBFs) for Multiagent-POMDPs. A technique based on barrier functions designs a minimally interfering safety-shield ensuring satisfaction of high-level specifications in terms of linear distribution temporal logic (LDTL). The approach uses discrete-time barrier functions (DTBFs) to enforce safety/mission specifications in Multiagent-POMDPs at runtime with minimum interference. For a function \mathcal{B}: X \to \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}, the safe set is \{x : \mathcal{B}(x) \ge 0\}, and the CBF condition is

\sup_{u \in \mathcal{U}} \left[ \frac{\partial \mathcal{B}}{\partial x}(f(x) + g(x)u) + \alpha_r(\mathcal{B}(x)) \right] \ge 0

for all x \in X, where \alpha_r is an extended class-\mathcal{K}^e function. The method can be implemented online by a sequence of one-step greedy algorithms.

Approach 2: Sampling-based Hamilton–Jacobi reachability. The viability kernel can be approximated via Lagrangian methods that avoid the exponential growth of computing time with dimension.

