7. Byzantine Agents and Adversarial Fault Tolerance

7.1 Byzantine Dec-POMDP

Definition 7.1 (Byzantine Dec-POMDP). A Byzantine Dec-POMDP extends Definition 2.7 with a fault specification:

\mathcal{D}_{\text{Byz}} = \langle \mathcal{D}, f, \mathcal{A}_{\text{Byz}} \rangle

where f \in [0, 1] is the fraction of Byzantine agents and \mathcal{A}_{\text{Byz}} is the set of admissible adversarial behaviors.

LLM-based agents demonstrate stronger skepticism when processing erroneous message flows, enabling them to outperform traditional agents across different topological structures. The CP-WBFT consensus mechanism capitalizes on the intrinsic reflective and discriminative capabilities of LLMs, achieving superior performance under extreme Byzantine conditions (85.7% fault rate).

7.2 Collaboration tax under Byzantine faults

Proposition 7.1 (Byzantine collaboration tax). For f > 0, the collaboration tax satisfies

\mathrm{CT}^{(N)}_{\text{Byz}} \ge \mathrm{CT}^{(N)} + f \cdot N \cdot \Delta_{\text{Byz}}

where \Delta_{\text{Byz}} > 0 is the per-agent degradation cost.

Proof. Byzantine agents contaminate otherwise reliable agents. Each Byzantine agent can sway neighboring agents toward incorrect conclusions, degrading overall system performance. The additional tax scales with f \cdot N. \blacksquare

7.3 Authority separation bounds Byzantine blast radius

Theorem 7.1 (Authority separation under Byzantine faults). With authority separation and (F+1)-robustness conditions on the communication graph, the blast radius satisfies

\mathbb{E}[B_{\text{Byz,sep}}] \le \mathbb{E}[B_{\text{Byz,unsep}}]

where F = \lfloor fN \rfloor.

Proof. (F+1)-robustness conditions ensure honest agents preserve and propagate reliable information despite Byzantine influence. Authority separation prevents Byzantine agents from executing unauthorized actions, reducing the effective branching factor. \blacksquare

