8. Phase Transition: Independent Conjecture with Effective Context Windows

Important distinction. Liu et al. prove a sharp phase transition for binary success/failure tasks with majority aggregation on deep b-ary trees with correlated inputs and lossy communication. A single scalar \alpha_\rho determines whether weak signal is amplified to a nontrivial fixed point or washed out to chance. In the amplifying regime, they derive an organization exponent s and show that budgeted synergy occurs exactly when s > \beta, yielding closed-form compute allocation rules and explicit budget thresholds. Their result is a regime boundary, not an agent-count cutoff derived from context-window-over-bandwidth.

Conjecture 8.1 (Agent-count phase transition with effective context). For multi-agent systems with effective context window W_{\text{eff}}(N), communication bandwidth B, and error correlation \rho, there exists a critical agent count satisfying the implicit equation

N^* = \frac{\log(W_{\text{eff}}(N^*)/B)}{\log(\alpha_\rho/\beta_\rho)}

such that for N < N^*, P_D > P_C, and for N > N^*, P_D < P_C.

Rationale. The effective context window W_{\text{eff}}(N) is a decreasing function of N: as more agents share communication history, the accumulated context triggers attention dilution and context rot. A model with a 200K token window can exhibit significant degradation at 50K tokens, with sudden rather than gradual performance drops. Three compounding mechanisms drive context rot: the lost-in-the-middle effect (30%+ accuracy drops), attention dilution (quadratic growth of pairwise relationships), and distractor interference. This makes the phase transition an implicit equation for N^*, explaining why adding agents eventually causes a sudden collapse in performance.

Status. This is a conjecture to be tested experimentally, not a theorem. It is consistent with the empirical observation that coordination benefits invert above a threshold, and with the phase-transition regime boundary of Liu et al., but the specific functional form must be validated or falsified. In the limit of perfect communication and infinite context (W_{\text{eff}} \to \infty, B \to \infty with W_{\text{eff}}/B fixed), the conjecture should recover the Liu et al. threshold s > \beta.

