9. Latency-Constrained Multi-Agent Systems: From Deep Space to Deep Sea

9.1 The extreme latency regime

Definition 9.1 (Latency-constrained Dec-POMDP). A latency-constrained multi-agent system has communication delays \tau_{ij} > 0 for inter-node links, with local links having \tau_{\text{local}} \approx 0.

Extreme latency regimes include:

Domain One-way delay Reference
Earth–Mars 4–22 minutes 
Earth–Moon ~1.3 seconds —
Deep-sea acoustic 0.5–5 seconds —
5G edge-cloud robotics 1–50 ms —

9.2 Local autonomy theorem

Theorem 9.1 (Local autonomy necessity). If \tau_{ij} > \tau_{\text{decision}} for all j in a different planetary system, then optimal policy requires local autonomy:

\pi_i^* = \arg\max_{\pi_i} \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_t \gamma^t R_t \mid o_i^t, \mathcal{C}_{\text{global}}^{t-\tau}\right]

where o_i^t are local observations and \mathcal{C}_{\text{global}}^{t-\tau} are delayed global constraints.

Proof. Any policy depending on observations delayed by \tau > \tau_{\text{decision}} cannot respond to state changes occurring within the decision window. The optimal policy must therefore condition on local observations, with delayed global constraints used asynchronously. \blacksquare

Corollary 9.2.1. The architecture for multiplanetary AI must satisfy

\text{Local Autonomy} + \text{Global Constraints} + \text{Asynchronous Coordination}

The Mars Avatar Project's Mission AI architecture provides a concrete instantiation: the one-way signal delay of 4–22 minutes is treated as a design condition rather than an obstacle: the operator transmits goals, and onboard autonomy determines how to achieve them. Mission AI is specified as a five-layer reference architecture with a formal goal-package protocol, graduated decision classes, and measurable quality metrics.

9.3 Two-timescale coordination

Definition 9.2 (Two-timescale coordination). Let \epsilon = \tau_{\text{local}}/\tau_{\text{global}}. Coordination operates at rate \epsilon t; local policies at rate t.

Theorem 9.2 (Stability under time-scale separation). If \epsilon \to 0, the hierarchical system converges to the solution of the global optimization problem.

Proof. This follows from singular perturbation theory. As \epsilon \to 0, the fast dynamics (local decisions) reach quasi-steady state for each fixed slow variable (global constraints). The slow dynamics then evolve according to the averaged system. \blacksquare

