10.2 Benchmark classes

Class Task type Predicted winner
A Independent subtasks \mathcal{D}
B Strongly coupled \mathcal{C}
C Long-horizon planning \mathcal{C}
D Scientific research \mathcal{H}
E Engineering design \mathcal{H}
F Crisis response \mathcal{D} with verification
G Planetary operations \mathcal{D}_{\text{lat}}
H Adversarial coordination \mathcal{C} or \mathcal{H} with BFT

10.3 Metrics

\mathcal{M} = \left\{P_{\text{task}}, R, L, K, C_{\text{comm}}, F, T_{\text{rec}}, E_{\text{coord}}, V_{\text{safe}}, V_{\text{auth}}, \mathbb{E}[B]\right\}

10.4 Falsification criteria

The framework is falsified if:

1. The lower bound of the 95% confidence interval for \mathrm{CT}^{(N)} falls below zero.
2. \mathrm{CT}^{(N+1)} < \mathrm{CT}^{(N)} is observed with statistical significance (p < 0.01).
3. No N^* observed across 100+ configurations.
4. The empirical distribution of blast radii \hat{B} fails a Kolmogorov–Smirnov test against the theoretical branching process distribution at p < 0.01.
5. Authority separation does not reduce V_{\text{auth}}.
6. P_H \le \max(P_C, P_D) across all tasks.
7. \mathrm{Viab}(\mathcal{S}_{\text{align}}) = \varnothing for realistic specifications.

10.5 Numerical example: Three-agent ring topology

To demonstrate the quantities in action, consider a three-agent ring topology with homogeneous propagation parameters p = 0.3, r = 1.5, and verification catching q = 0.5 of errors.

Signed next-generation matrix:

K = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0.45 & 0 \\ 0.45 & 0 & 0.45 \\ 0 & 0.45 & 0 \end{pmatrix}

Absolute-value matrix:

|K| = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0.45 & 0 \\ 0.45 & 0 & 0.45 \\ 0 & 0.45 & 0 \end{pmatrix}

Spectral radius: \rho(|K|) = 0.45\sqrt{2} \approx 0.636.

Verification-adjusted reproduction number: R_c^{\text{ver}} = (1-0.5) \times 0.636 = 0.318.

Expected blast radius:

\mathbb{E}[B] = \mathbf{1}^T (I - |K^{\text{ver}}|)^{-1} \mathbf{1} \approx 1 + 0.318 + 0.101 + 0.032 + \cdots \approx 1.48

Interpretation. With verification catching 50% of errors, the effective reproduction number is below the critical threshold R_c = 1, and the expected cascade size is finite and small. Without verification, R_c = 0.636 < 1, so cascades are still subcritical but larger. This example demonstrates that the spectral framework yields concrete, computable quantities for small systems.

