A.1 Proof of Proposition 3.1 (Monotonicity with Context Dilution)

Proposition 3.1. For fixed communication topology and fixed effective context length L_{\text{eff}}, the collaboration tax \mathrm{CT}^{(N)} is non-decreasing in N:

\mathrm{CT}^{(N+1)} \ge \mathrm{CT}^{(N)}

provided that (i) the new agent's private information is not already spanned by existing agents' information, (ii) communication constraints remain fixed, and (iii) the full-information value increases at least as fast as the decentralized value. Under context dilution, the inequality is strengthened:

\mathrm{CT}^{(N+1)} \ge \mathrm{CT}^{(N)} + \delta(N) \cdot \Delta V

Proof.

Let \mathcal{I}^{(N)} = \sigma(o_1, \ldots, o_N) denote the information partition generated by N agents' observations. By the theory of team decision problems, the optimal team value V(\mathcal{G}) is a function of the information partition:

V(\mathcal{G}) = \sup_{\pi_1, \ldots, \pi_N} \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=0}^{\infty} \gamma^t R(s_t, a_t) \mid \mathcal{I}^{(N)}\right]

Step 1: Information partition refinement. Adding agent N+1 with private information o_{N+1} refines the partition:

\mathcal{I}^{(N+1)} = \mathcal{I}^{(N)} \vee \sigma(o_{N+1})

where \vee denotes the join of \sigma-algebras. By assumption (i), o_{N+1} is not \mathcal{I}^{(N)}-measurable, so \mathcal{I}^{(N+1)} \supsetneq \mathcal{I}^{(N)}.

Step 2: Full-information value. The full-information value is

V(\mathcal{G}_{\text{full}}) = \sup_{\pi_1, \ldots, \pi_N} \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{t=0}^{\infty} \gamma^t R(s_t, a_t) \mid \mathcal{F}\right]

where \mathcal{F} is the complete information partition. Since \mathcal{I}^{(N+1)} \subseteq \mathcal{F}, we have

V(\mathcal{G}_{\text{full}}) \ge V(\mathcal{G}^{(N+1)}) \ge V(\mathcal{G}^{(N)})

Step 3: Collaboration tax comparison. The collaboration tax difference is

\mathrm{CT}^{(N+1)} - \mathrm{CT}^{(N)} = \left[V(\mathcal{G}_{\text{full}}) - V(\mathcal{G}^{(N+1)})\right] - \left[V(\mathcal{G}_{\text{full}}) - V(\mathcal{G}^{(N)})\right]

= V(\mathcal{G}^{(N)}) - V(\mathcal{G}^{(N+1)})

By the theory of team decision problems (Marschak, 1955), the value of a decentralized team with finer information partitions is bounded above by the value with coarser partitions. Therefore:

V(\mathcal{G}^{(N)}) \ge V(\mathcal{G}^{(N+1)})

which gives \mathrm{CT}^{(N+1)} \ge \mathrm{CT}^{(N)}.

Step 4: Context dilution strengthening. Under context dilution, the effective information available to each agent is reduced. The softmax attention weights satisfy

\alpha_{ij}^{(N)} = \frac{\exp(q_i^\top k_j / \sqrt{d})}{\sum_{\ell=1}^{N}\exp(q_i^\top k_\ell / \sqrt{d})}

As N increases, the denominator grows, and each \alpha_{ij}^{(N)} decreases. The effective mutual information between agent i's observation and the shared history satisfies

I(o_i^t; \mathcal{H}_t^{\text{shared}}) \le \frac{C}{N}

for some constant C depending on the attention mechanism. Therefore, the effective information partition \mathcal{I}^{(N)}_{\text{eff}} satisfies

\mathcal{I}^{(N+1)}_{\text{eff}} \subseteq \mathcal{I}^{(N)}_{\text{eff}} \vee \sigma(o_{N+1}) \text{ with reduced resolution}

The value gap V(\mathcal{G}_{\text{full}}) - V(\mathcal{G}^{(N)}) grows by at least

\Delta V \cdot \delta(N) = \Delta V \cdot \left[1 - \exp\left(-\frac{L(N)}{L_{\text{eff}}}\right)\right]

which yields the strengthened inequality. \blacksquare

