A.2 Proof of Proposition 3.2 (Topology Dependence)

Proposition 3.2. For fixed N,

\mathrm{CT}^{(N)}(\mathcal{G}_{\text{complete}}) \le \mathrm{CT}^{(N)}(\mathcal{G}_{\text{sparse}})

provided communication is free, perfect, and instantaneous.

Proof.

Under free, perfect, instantaneous communication, the complete graph \mathcal{G}_{\text{complete}} allows all agents to share all information. Therefore, the decentralized value under \mathcal{G}_{\text{complete}} equals the full-information value:

V(\mathcal{G}_{\text{complete}}) = V(\mathcal{G}_{\text{full}})

Hence:

\mathrm{CT}^{(N)}(\mathcal{G}_{\text{complete}}) = V(\mathcal{G}_{\text{full}}) - V(\mathcal{G}_{\text{full}}) = 0

For any sparser graph \mathcal{G}_{\text{sparse}} \subsetneq \mathcal{G}_{\text{complete}}, information is restricted, so

V(\mathcal{G}_{\text{sparse}}) \le V(\mathcal{G}_{\text{full}})

Therefore:

\mathrm{CT}^{(N)}(\mathcal{G}_{\text{sparse}}) = V(\mathcal{G}_{\text{full}}) - V(\mathcal{G}_{\text{sparse}}) \ge 0 = \mathrm{CT}^{(N)}(\mathcal{G}_{\text{complete}})

\blacksquare

Remark A.2.1. When communication is costly or noisy, the complete graph may not achieve the full-information value because messages may be corrupted or delayed. In this case, the inequality may reverse.

