A.4 Proof of Theorem 4.1 (Derived Saturation Law)

Theorem 4.1. Under the collaboration-tax and context-dilution models, multi-agent performance follows

P_D(N) = p_0 + \alpha(1-p_0)N - \frac{\beta}{2}N^2 + \epsilon

where \alpha, \beta > 0, and the optimal agent count is

N^* = \frac{\alpha(1-p_0)}{\beta}

Proof.

From Theorem 3.1, the net gain from distribution is

\Delta P(N) = G_{\text{spec}} + G_{\text{parallel}} - \mathrm{CT}^{(N)} - K_{\text{coord}} - \Delta_{\text{verify}} - \Delta_{\text{resource}}

Step 1: Coordination cost scaling. The coordination cost and verification overhead scale linearly with N:

K_{\text{coord}} + \Delta_{\text{verify}} = \kappa N + \mathcal{O}(1)

where \kappa > 0 is the per-agent coordination cost.

Step 2: Collaboration tax scaling. From Proposition 3.1, the collaboration tax grows super-linearly due to context dilution:

\mathrm{CT}^{(N)} = \tau_0 + \tau_1 N + \tau_2 N^2 + \mathcal{O}(N^3)

where \tau_2 > 0 captures the quadratic effect of attention dilution.

Step 3: Substitution. Substituting into the distribution condition:

P_D(N) = P_C + G_{\text{spec}} + G_{\text{parallel}} - \tau_0 - (\tau_1 + \kappa)N - \tau_2 N^2 + \mathcal{O}(N^3)

Step 4: Parameter identification. Set

p_0 = P_C + G_{\text{spec}} + G_{\text{parallel}} - \tau_0

\alpha(1-p_0) = -(\tau_1 + \kappa)

\frac{\beta}{2} = \tau_2

For small N, the cubic and higher-order terms are negligible, yielding

P_D(N) = p_0 + \alpha(1-p_0)N - \frac{\beta}{2}N^2 + \epsilon

Step 5: Optimal agent count. Taking the derivative:

\frac{\partial P_D}{\partial N} = \alpha(1-p_0) - \beta N = 0

N^* = \frac{\alpha(1-p_0)}{\beta}

\blacksquare

