A.5 Proof of Theorem 4.2 (Communication Threshold)

Theorem 4.2. There exists B^* such that B > B^* \implies P_D > P_C and B < B^* \implies P_D < P_C.

Proof.

Define the performance difference as a function of bandwidth B:

f(B) = P_D(B) - P_C

Step 1: Behavior at low bandwidth. As B \to 0, the coordination cost K_{\text{coord}} \to \infty because

\kappa_{ij} = \alpha \cdot \frac{|m_{ij}|}{B_{ij}} + \beta \cdot \tau_{ij} + \gamma \cdot \epsilon_{ij}

Therefore f(B) \to -\infty < 0.

Step 2: Behavior at high bandwidth. As B \to \infty, K_{\text{coord}} \to \beta \tau + \gamma \epsilon, and the collaboration tax decreases because faster communication reduces information staleness:

\frac{\partial \mathrm{CT}^{(N)}}{\partial B} < 0

Therefore:

\lim_{B \to \infty} f(B) = G_{\text{spec}} + G_{\text{parallel}} - \mathrm{CT}^{(N)}(\infty) - \beta\tau - \gamma\epsilon - \Delta_{\text{verify}} - \Delta_{\text{resource}}

Assuming the specialization and parallelization gains exceed the residual costs, f(B) \to G_{\text{spec}} + G_{\text{parallel}} > 0.

Step 3: Intermediate value theorem. Since f is continuous in B, there exists B^* such that f(B^*) = 0. For B > B^*, f(B) > 0; for B < B^*, f(B) < 0. \blacksquare

