A.8 Proof of Corollary 5.2.1 (Topology Scaling)

Corollary 5.2.1. For fixed total agents N and homogeneous propagation parameters p, r:

Topology R_c scaling \mathbb{E}[B] scaling
Centralized R_c \propto N \cdot p \cdot r 1/(1 - Npr)
Hierarchical R_c \propto \log N \cdot p \cdot r 1/(1 - pr\log N)
Ring R_c \propto 2pr 1/(1 - 2pr)

Proof.

Centralized topology. The hub agent connects to all N peripheral agents. The next-generation matrix K has:

· K_{\text{hub},j} = p \cdot r for all j (hub infects any peripheral)
· K_{j,\text{hub}} = p \cdot r for all j (any peripheral infects hub)

The row sum for the hub is N \cdot p \cdot r. By the Perron–Frobenius theorem for non-negative matrices, the spectral radius equals the maximum row sum for a stochastic-like matrix:

R_c = N \cdot p \cdot r

The expected blast radius follows from Theorem 5.1:

\mathbb{E}[B] = \frac{1}{1 - Npr}

Hierarchical topology. In a balanced binary tree with N nodes, the depth is \lceil \log_2 N \rceil. A cascade must traverse each level, and at each level, the branching factor is at most 2 (each node has at most 2 children). The effective reproduction number is:

R_c = p \cdot r \cdot \log_2 N

The expected blast radius is:

\mathbb{E}[B] = \frac{1}{1 - pr\log_2 N}

Ring topology. Each node has degree 2 (one predecessor, one successor). The next-generation matrix has row sums 2pr. Therefore:

R_c = 2pr

\mathbb{E}[B] = \frac{1}{1 - 2pr}

\blacksquare

