A.9 Proof of Theorem 6.1 (Collective Alignment)

Theorem 6.1. A multi-agent system is collectively aligned under policy \pi iff the trajectory \{X_t\}_{t \ge 0} satisfies X_t \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{align}} for all t \ge 0. A system admits an aligned policy iff

X_0 \in \mathrm{Viab}(\mathcal{S}_{\text{align}})

Proof.

Part 1: Fixed-policy alignment. By Definition 2.11, a global alignment specification is a set \mathcal{S}_{\text{align}} \subseteq \mathcal{X} with the viability constraint:

X_0 \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{align}} \implies X_t \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{align}} \quad \forall t \ge 0

Under a fixed policy \pi, the trajectory \{X_t\}_{t \ge 0} is determined. The system is aligned under \pi iff this trajectory satisfies the constraint. This is the definition of fixed-policy alignment.

Part 2: Existence of an aligned policy. The viability kernel is defined as:

\mathrm{Viab}(\mathcal{S}_{\text{align}}) = \{X_0 \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{align}} : \exists \pi, \forall t \ge 0, X_t \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{align}}\}

By definition, X_0 \in \mathrm{Viab}(\mathcal{S}_{\text{align}}) iff there exists a policy \pi such that the trajectory remains in \mathcal{S}_{\text{align}} for all t \ge 0. This is exactly the condition for the existence of an aligned policy. \blacksquare

