A.14 Proof of Theorem 9.1 (Local Autonomy Necessity)

Theorem 9.1. If \tau_{ij} > \tau_{\text{decision}} for all j in a different planetary system, then optimal policy requires local autonomy:

\pi_i^* = \arg\max_{\pi_i} \mathbb{E}\left[\sum_t \gamma^t R_t \mid o_i^t, \mathcal{C}_{\text{global}}^{t-\tau}\right]

Proof.

Step 1: Delayed observation. Any policy depending on observations delayed by \tau > \tau_{\text{decision}} cannot respond to state changes occurring within the decision window. Formally, if the policy uses o_j^{t-\tau} with \tau > \tau_{\text{decision}}, then the action a_i^t is based on information that is at least \tau time steps old.

Step 2: Decision window. The decision window is the maximum time within which an action must be taken to affect the current state. If \tau > \tau_{\text{decision}}, the state has changed by the time the delayed observation is received.

Step 3: Optimal policy. The optimal policy must therefore condition on local observations o_i^t, which are available in real-time. Delayed global constraints \mathcal{C}_{\text{global}}^{t-\tau} can be used asynchronously to guide the policy but cannot be the primary basis for action. \blacksquare

