A.15 Proof of Theorem 9.2 (Two-Timescale Convergence)

Theorem 9.2. If \epsilon \to 0, the hierarchical system converges to the solution of the global optimization problem.

Proof.

Step 1: Singular perturbation. Consider the two-timescale system:

\frac{dx}{dt} = f(x, y, \epsilon)

\epsilon \frac{dy}{dt} = g(x, y, \epsilon)

where x represents slow variables (global constraints) and y represents fast variables (local decisions).

Step 2: Boundary layer. As \epsilon \to 0, the fast dynamics reach quasi-steady state:

g(x, y, 0) = 0 \implies y = h(x)

Step 3: Reduced system. Substituting into the slow dynamics:

\frac{dx}{dt} = f(x, h(x), 0)

Step 4: Averaging. By the averaging theorem for singularly perturbed systems, the solution of the full system converges to the solution of the reduced system as \epsilon \to 0. Therefore, the hierarchical system converges to the solution of the global optimization problem. \blacksquare

