Appendix B: Perron–Frobenius and Metzler Matrix Theory

B.1 Perron–Frobenius Theorem

Theorem B.1 (Perron–Frobenius). Let A \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times n} be a non-negative matrix (A_{ij} \ge 0 for all i, j).

1. There exists a real eigenvalue \rho(A) \ge 0 such that \rho(A) \ge |\lambda| for all eigenvalues \lambda of A.
2. There exists a non-negative eigenvector v \ge 0 such that Av = \rho(A)v.
3. If A is irreducible, then \rho(A) > 0 and v > 0.

Application to cascade threshold. For the next-generation matrix K with non-negative entries, R_c = \rho(K). If R_c < 1, the branching process is subcritical and the expected cascade size is finite.

B.2 Metzler Matrices

Definition B.1 (Metzler matrix). A matrix A \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times n} is Metzler if all off-diagonal elements are non-negative:

A_{ij} \ge 0 \quad \forall i \neq j

Theorem B.2 (Metzler stability). A Metzler matrix A is stable (all eigenvalues have negative real part) iff there exists a positive vector v > 0 such that Av < 0.

Application to signed propagation. For a signed next-generation matrix K, we bound the spectral radius using the absolute value matrix:

\rho(K) \le \rho(|K|)

If |K| is irreducible and non-negative, Perron–Frobenius applies to |K|, and the cascade threshold is governed by \rho(|K|).

B.3 Spectral Abscissa

Definition B.2 (Spectral abscissa). For a matrix A, the spectral abscissa is

\alpha(A) = \max_{i} \text{Re}(\lambda_i)

where \lambda_i are the eigenvalues of A.

Remark B.3.1. For Metzler matrices, the spectral abscissa can be computed via the Collatz–Wielandt formula:

\alpha(A) = \max_{v > 0} \min_{i : v_i > 0} \frac{(Av)_i}{v_i}

This provides a sharper bound than \rho(|K|) when many p_{ij} < 0.

