Appendix C: Context Dilution and Attention Mechanics

C.1 Softmax Attention

Definition C.1 (Scaled dot-product attention). For query q_i, key k_j, and value v_j:

\text{Attention}(Q, K, V) = \text{softmax}\left(\frac{QK^\top}{\sqrt{d}}\right)V

The attention weight from token i to token j is

\alpha_{ij} = \frac{\exp(q_i^\top k_j / \sqrt{d})}{\sum_{\ell=1}^{N}\exp(q_i^\top k_\ell / \sqrt{d})}

C.2 Attention Dilution

Proposition C.1 (Attention dilution). As the number of tokens N increases, the expected attention weight per token decreases:

\mathbb{E}[\alpha_{ij}] = \frac{1}{N}

Proof. By symmetry, if all keys are equally similar to the query, then

\alpha_{ij} = \frac{1}{N}

for all j. In practice, attention is concentrated on a few tokens, but the sum of all weights is 1, so the average weight is 1/N. \blacksquare

C.3 Effective Context Length

Definition C.2 (Effective context length). The effective context length L_{\text{eff}} is the maximum number of tokens beyond which model accuracy degrades below a threshold \theta:

L_{\text{eff}} = \max\{L : \text{Acc}(L) \ge \theta\}

Empirical observation. Chroma Research (2025) found that a model with a 200K token window can exhibit significant degradation at 50K tokens, with the decline being continuous rather than a cliff.

C.4 Context Dilution Factor

Definition C.3 (Context dilution factor). For N agents sharing a communication history of length L(N):

\delta(N) = 1 - \exp\left(-\frac{L(N)}{L_{\text{eff}}}\right)

Properties:

1. \delta(0) = 0
2. \delta(N) \to 1 as N \to \infty
3. \delta'(N) > 0 (monotonically increasing)

