Appendix D: Control Barrier Functions for Multiagent-POMDPs

D.1 Discrete-Time Barrier Functions

Definition D.1 (Discrete-time barrier function). A function \mathcal{B}: X \to \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0} is a discrete-time barrier function (DTBF) for the safe set \mathcal{S}_{\text{align}} = \{x : \mathcal{B}(x) \ge 0\} if there exists a class-\mathcal{K} function \alpha such that

\mathcal{B}(x_{t+1}) \ge (1-\alpha)\mathcal{B}(x_t)

for all x_t \in X.

D.2 Multiagent-POMDP Construction

Theorem D.1 (DTBF for Multiagent-POMDPs). For a Multiagent-POMDP with LDTL specifications, there exists a DTBF \mathcal{B} such that the safety shield

\pi_{\text{safe}}(x) = \arg\min_{u \in \mathcal{U}} \|u - \pi(x)\|^2

subject to

\mathcal{B}(f(x, u)) \ge (1-\alpha)\mathcal{B}(x)

ensures satisfaction of the safety specification at runtime.

Proof. See Ahmadi et al. (2020) for the full construction. The key idea is to encode the LDTL specification as a barrier function and solve a quadratic program at each time step. \blacksquare

D.3 Computational Complexity

Proposition D.1. The DTBF-based safety shield has computational complexity \mathcal{O}(N \cdot |\mathcal{U}|), where N is the number of agents and |\mathcal{U}| is the size of the action space.

---

Appendix E: Byzantine Fault Tolerance for Multi-Agent LLMs

E.1 Byzantine Generals Problem

Definition E.1 (Byzantine fault). An agent is Byzantine if it can exhibit arbitrary behavior, including:

· Sending conflicting messages to different agents
· Failing to send messages
· Sending corrupted messages

E.2 CP-WBFT Consensus

Definition E.2 (CP-WBFT). Confidence Probe-based Weighted Byzantine Fault Tolerance (CP-WBFT) is a consensus mechanism that:

1. Uses confidence probes to assess agent reliability
2. Applies weighted voting based on confidence scores
3. Achieves consensus even under extreme Byzantine conditions

Empirical result. Zheng et al. (2026) found that CP-WBFT achieves superior performance under extreme Byzantine conditions (85.7% fault rate).

E.3 (F+1)-Robustness

Definition E.3 ((F+1)-robustness). A communication graph is (F+1)-robust if for any subset S of at most F Byzantine agents, the set of honest agents has at least F+1 paths to every other honest agent.

Theorem E.1. Under (F+1)-robustness, honest agents can preserve and propagate reliable information despite Byzantine influence.

Proof. See Zheng et al. (2026) for the full proof. \blacksquare

