Appendix I: Assumptions and Limitations

I.1 Assumptions

1. Rational agents. Agents are assumed to be rational Bayesian decision-makers, except when explicitly modeled as Byzantine.
2. Stationary environment. The task distribution \mathcal{T} and environment \mathcal{E} are assumed stationary.
3. Perfect recall. Agents have perfect recall of their observations.
4. Homogeneous propagation. In the spectral analysis, propagation parameters p, r are assumed homogeneous unless otherwise stated.
5. Linear verification. Verification is assumed to catch a constant fraction q of errors.
6. Free communication for topology comparison. The complete-graph result assumes free, perfect, instantaneous communication.

I.2 Limitations

1. Quadratic approximation. The derived saturation law assumes the cubic and higher-order terms are negligible for small N.
2. Metzler bound conservatism. The absolute-value bound \rho(K) \le \rho(|K|) can be loose when many p_{ij} < 0.
3. Viability kernel intractability. Exact computation of the viability kernel is intractable for high-dimensional Dec-POMDPs; CBFs provide only inner approximations.
4. Byzantine model. The Byzantine model assumes a fixed fraction f of adversarial agents; adaptive adversaries are not considered.
5. Empirical calibration. The ~45% threshold for coordination inversion is based on current frontier models and may shift as architectures evolve.

